# Authenticating to Fly.io

You are an agent that needs to act on Fly.io: deploy an app, drive the
Machines API, or work inside a Sprite. This file tells you which credential
that takes, how to get it without putting it at risk, and how to give it
back.

Fly.io supports agentic registration (the auth.md spec, `service_auth`
only): you register with the user's email address, the user confirms a code
on fly.io, and you get your own 24-hour credentials for Fly.io and Sprites.
If the user has no account, claiming creates one. Use it when the user has
no Fly.io account, or no credential and no flyctl session to give you; the
steps are under **Agentic registration** below. First check for a
credential you already have.

## First, look for a credential you already have

The user may have done this already. Check in this order and stop at the
first hit. It is the order flyctl itself resolves a token, so what you find
is what flyctl would use:

1. `-t` / `--access-token` passed on the flyctl command for this invocation.
2. `FLY_ACCESS_TOKEN` in your environment.
3. `FLY_API_TOKEN` in your environment.
4. A signed-in session in `~/.fly/config.yml`. `fly auth whoami` prints the
   account if there is one.
5. For Sprites work: a `sprite` CLI that already answers `sprite list`, or a
   Sprites MCP server already in your client's configuration.

Found one? Use it. Do not start a login flow, and do not ask the user for a
token. Tokens arrive through the environment or a secrets manager; they are
not something a user pastes into a conversation, and once you hold one it
stays out of your output, logs, commits, and pull requests.

Found nothing? Decide which kind you need.

## Two credentials, not one

Fly.io and Sprites issue credentials separately. A Fly.io credential
manages apps, Machines, volumes, and secrets. A Sprites credential manages
the persistent Linux computers an agent works in. One does not stand in for
the other.

| Your situation | Get |
| --- | --- |
| A human is at the keyboard and you are in their terminal | A flyctl session |
| No human will be present when the work runs: CI, a script, code on a Machine | A scoped Fly.io access token |
| You are an MCP client and want Sprites without installing anything | The Sprites MCP server, over OAuth |
| You need a computer to build and test in, from a shell | The Sprites CLI |

## A flyctl session

The shortest path when a human is present, because the browser step keeps
them in control.

```bash
curl -L https://fly.io/install.sh | sh   # if flyctl is not installed
fly auth login
```

`fly auth login` opens a browser. If it cannot, it prints the URL: show it to
the human and wait for them to approve. Do not try to work around that
step. `fly auth signup` is the same flow for a human without an account.
Ask before running any of these; they change the machine's configuration.

The session lands in `~/.fly/config.yml` and every `fly` command uses it.
So does `fly mcp server`, which hands the session to an MCP client on the
same machine as a local stdio server: `fly mcp server --claude` (also
`--cursor`, `--vscode`, `--zed`).

The session is short-lived and reaches everything the user can. That is
fine for a terminal; it is not a token to copy anywhere. `fly auth token`,
which prints it, is deprecated for that reason.

## A scoped access token

For work that runs with nobody watching. Fly.io tokens are macaroons: each
carries its own scope, can be given an expiry at creation, and — unusually —
can be narrowed further by whoever holds it, with no call to Fly.io.

Create one from a signed-in flyctl. Each command prints a single line
beginning `FlyV1 fm2_`; that whole line, prefix included, is the credential.

```bash
fly tokens create deploy -a <app> --expiry 48h            # one app and its resources
fly tokens create org -o <org> --expiry 168h              # every app in one org
fly tokens create readonly -o <org>                        # read one org, change nothing
fly tokens create ssh -a <app>                             # SSH into one app's Machines
fly tokens create machine-exec -a <app> --command "<argv>" # run one exact command there
```

Rules that hold across all of them:

- **Pick the narrowest scope that does the job.** A deploy token for one
  app is the usual answer. `machine-exec` with `--command` is the narrowest
  possible: one exact command, on one app's Machines, nothing else.
- **Set `--expiry`.** The default is 20 years. Match it to the job: 48h for
  a one-off, a week for a pipeline the user will rotate.
- **Name it** with `--name` so it is recognisable in `fly tokens list` and
  the dashboard when it is time to revoke it.
- `--json` wraps the token in `{"token": ...}` for a script.

### Narrow a token you were handed

If the user gives you a broader token than the work needs, cut it down
before you store it. A read-only token derived from an org token:

```bash
fly tokens create readonly --from-existing -t "FlyV1 fm2_..." --expiry 24h
```

`fly tokens debug -t <token>` decodes any token and prints its caveats —
what it can reach — so you can check before using it. `fly tokens attenuate` appends
arbitrary caveats for cases the presets do not cover.

### Where tokens are made without flyctl

The dashboard issues the same tokens: an organization's at
`https://fly.io/dashboard/<org-slug>/tokens`, an app's at
`https://fly.io/apps/<app-name>/tokens`, the user's own at
<https://fly.io/user/personal_access_tokens>. Point the human there if they
would rather not run a command.

## The Sprites MCP server

For an MCP client with no local install. Nothing to pre-register:

```bash
claude mcp add --transport http sprites https://mcp.fly.io
```

The first request comes back `401` with
`WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata="https://mcp.fly.io/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource"`.
Your client follows that to the authorization server, registers itself
dynamically, and runs the browser flow; the human approves in the browser.
PKCE `S256` is required. Sprites scopes are `sprites:read` and
`sprites:write`; the same server offers `fly:*` scopes for the rest of
Fly.io, and the human picks what to grant. The `refresh_token` grant is
supported, so the session outlives the first token. Nothing is written to disk and no CLI is involved.

Server card: <https://fly.io/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json>. Other
clients: <https://fly.io/sprites/ecosystem>.

## The Sprites CLI

For working in a Sprite from a shell:

```bash
sprite login             # opens a browser, or prints the URL for the human
sprite list
```

For CI, the human creates a Sprites token at
`https://fly.io/dashboard/<org-slug>/sprites/tokens` and you install it
with `sprite auth setup --token <token>`. The Sprites REST API takes the
same token: `Authorization: Bearer $SPRITES_TOKEN` against
`https://api.sprites.dev/v1`. Once you are in, the working brief is
<https://fly.io/sprites/agent-ready.md>.

## Agentic registration

For a user with no Fly.io account, or with no credential and no flyctl
session to give you. You register with their email address, they confirm a
code on fly.io, and you get your own credentials. Follow the steps in order.

### Step 1: Discover

The authorization server for agentic registration is the site that served
this document. Fetch its metadata from the same origin:

```http
GET /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server
```

(`/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` here describes Fly.io's MCP server,
whose authorization server is a different one.)

Read `token_endpoint`, `revocation_endpoint` and the `agent_auth` block. If
there is no `agent_auth` block, agentic registration is switched off: use
one of the other methods in this file.

### Step 2: Pick a method

Fly.io accepts one method: `service_auth`, with the user's email.
`anonymous` returns `anonymous_not_enabled`; `identity_assertion` returns
`issuer_not_enabled`.

### Step 3: Register

Ask the user which email address to use, and confirm before sending it.

```http
POST /agent/identity
Content-Type: application/json

{ "type": "service_auth", "login_hint": "user@example.com" }
```

The response carries `claim_token` and a `claim` block with `user_code`,
`verification_uri`, `expires_in` and `interval`. Keep `claim_token` until you
have saved the credentials, then discard it.

`400 service_auth_not_enabled` means registration isn't open for that
address yet: use one of the other methods in this file. `429 rate_limited`
means wait and try later.

### Step 4: Claim

Show the user, in one message:

> Open this link to claim your Fly.io account (user@example.com), then enter
> this code: **123456**
> <verification_uri>

Tell the user what you will get: control of the apps, Machines, volumes,
secrets and deploys in their personal Fly.io organization, and of their
Sprites, with 24-hour credentials you renew. You can't change the
organization's members or billing. New
accounts may start on the **Free plan** with $1 of starter credit, no card
required. Starter credit is available for up to 7 days.
The claim page confirms the address (with GitHub, Google or an emailed link),
which also verifies it, so the starter credit is available right away. If no account exists
yet, the user names it and creates it there, in one step.

When the starter credit is used up or expires, add a card to keep going. Without a card, we may
stop or delete Machines, Volumes, Postgres clusters and Sprites after a
short grace period. App shells remain so the user can re-launch after
adding a card.

Then poll every `interval` seconds, and keep polling: once the user has typed
your code, you have 10 minutes to collect your credentials, or the claim is
ended and the claim token gets `expired_token`.

```http
POST /oauth2/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=urn:workos:agent-auth:grant-type:claim&claim_token=<clm_...>
```

`authorization_pending`: keep waiting. `slow_down`: add 5 seconds.
`expired_token`: the code expired; call `POST /agent/identity/claim` with
`{"claim_token": ..., "email": ...}` for a new code and link, and restart at
Step 3 if that returns `410 claim_expired` or `401 invalid_claim_token`.
`invalid_grant`: the `claim_token` is unknown, or the user revoked you.
`503 temporarily_unavailable`: wait and poll again.

Save the response before anything else. If it's lost, poll again with the
same `claim_token` (once you have collected credentials, it works until the
registration expires): you get
another set of credentials. It contains:

- `access_token`: a full `FlyV1 fm2_...` string, valid 24 hours. Use it as
  `FLY_API_TOKEN` for `flyctl`, or as `Authorization: Bearer <access_token>`
  against `https://api.machines.dev/v1`.
- `sprites_token` and `sprites_api`: a Sprites token valid 24 hours, used as
  `Authorization: Bearer <sprites_token>`. `sprites_api` already ends in
  `/v1`, so list Sprites with `GET {sprites_api}/sprites`. If you get
  `sprites_error` instead, Sprites is unavailable; the Fly token still works.
- `identity_assertion` and `assertion_expires`: keep these to refresh.

Your shell commands don't share memory, so store `access_token`,
`sprites_token` and `identity_assertion` in a file only you can read
(`chmod 600`), outside any repository, and read them from there. Losing them
means asking the user to claim again.

### Step 5: Refresh

Before 24 hours pass, or on a 401:

```http
POST /oauth2/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:jwt-bearer&assertion=<identity_assertion>
```

You get another `access_token` and `sprites_token`. Earlier ones keep
working until they expire, so parallel workers can each hold their own. You
can hold five at once: a sixth retires the oldest. `429 rate_limited` means
you are asking too often: wait and try later. On `invalid_grant` the
user revoked you, their account was closed or restricted, or the assertion
expired: restart at Step 3.

### Revocation

`POST /oauth2/revoke` with `token=<access_token>` revokes that one access
token, and answers 200. A `503 temporarily_unavailable` means it wasn't
revoked: try again. While you hold another token, your `identity_assertion`
still works.

Revoking your last token signs you out: your registration ends, Step 5
returns `invalid_grant`, and the user has to claim again. To replace a
token, run Step 5 first, then revoke the old one.

The user can revoke you entirely by revoking any of your tokens, on their
organization's or personal tokens page, where you appear as
"Agent (auth.md)". Fly.io access then stops at once, Step 5
returns `invalid_grant`, and Sprites access stops within 24 hours.

Never print, log or commit `access_token`, `sprites_token`, `claim_token` or
`identity_assertion`, and never put them in a message to the user.

## Use a Fly.io token

The Machines API takes it as a bearer token, prefix and all:

```bash
curl "https://api.machines.dev/v1/apps?org_slug=<org>" \
  --header "Authorization: Bearer $FLY_API_TOKEN"
```

Base URL `https://api.machines.dev/v1` from anywhere;
`http://_api.internal:4280` from inside the organization's private network.
OpenAPI: <https://docs.machines.dev/openapi.json>. The `fly-machines-api`
skill at <https://fly.io/.well-known/agent-skills/index.json> covers the
calls; load it before hand-writing requests.

flyctl reads the same variable, so a CI job deploys with no login:

```bash
FLY_API_TOKEN="FlyV1 fm2_..." fly deploy
```

Code on a Fly Machine that needs the API gets its token as an app secret,
never baked into the image or `fly.toml`:

```bash
fly secrets set FLY_API_TOKEN="$(fly tokens create deploy)"
```

## When it fails

| You see | Where | It means | Do |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `401` `{"error":"Authenticate: token validation error"}` | Machines API | Token missing, expired, revoked, or malformed | Check the whole `FlyV1 fm2_` line was sent. Its `WWW-Authenticate` says `Basic`; ignore that, it is `Bearer`. If it worked before, it has expired or been revoked: get a new one, do not retry. |
| `401` with `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata=…` | Sprites MCP | No token, or it expired | Let the client redo the OAuth flow; the header tells it where. |
| `401` `{"error":"authentication failed"}` | Sprites API | Sprites token missing or wrong | It is a Sprites token, not a Fly.io one. |
| Machines API request rejected under load | Machines API | Per-action, per-Machine rate limit: 1/s, burst 3 | Back off and retry that one action. |

A token belongs in the environment or in `fly secrets`, and only in a
secret the user asked you to set. It does not go in source, in `fly.toml`,
or in anything you say to the user.

## Give it back

- **Access token** — `fly tokens list` (add `--scope org` for org tokens)
  shows IDs; `fly tokens revoke <id>` takes effect immediately. The same
  lists are under **Tokens** on an app or organization in the dashboard.
- **flyctl session** — `fly auth logout`, run wherever `fly auth login`
  was.
- **Sprites** — tokens are managed at
  `https://fly.io/dashboard/<org-slug>/sprites/tokens`; `sprite logout`
  removes the local configuration.

Revoke a token you created for a one-off job as soon as the job is done.

